"AMD GCN3 scoped axiomatic model (HetLitmus GPU-only oracle, Task 7)"

(*
 * Axiomatic (herd7) model for the HetLitmus GPU-only litmus corpus, targeting
 * the AMD GCN3 + x86 oracle of the PLDI'23 Compound Memory Models artifact
 * (gem5 build target GCN3_X86). Pairs with hetlitmus/bells/ptx.bell.
 *
 * GOAL: reproduce hetlitmus/tests/gpu-only/expected-amd-gcn3.csv (8/8).
 *
 * LINEAGE: derived from the in-tree HRF formalism
 *   (catalogue/hrf/cats/{hrfd,hrfi}.cat, Hower et al. "Heterogeneous-Race-Free
 *   Memory Models", ASPLOS'14), which is the scoped AMD/OpenCL race-freedom
 *   model. HRF is a race-FREEDOM model (`undefined_unless empty race`); this
 *   corpus is deliberately racy, so we keep HRF's scoped release->acquire
 *   synchronisation backbone (so / heterogeneous-happens-before) but turn it
 *   into an ALLOWABILITY model that emits Allowed/Forbidden. The NVIDIA
 *   catalogue/demo/cats/ptx.cat was consulted for STRUCTURE only; it is
 *   fence-based pure-ordering and computes SB/IRIW wrong for AMD, so it is not
 *   ported (see hetlitmus/docs/gpu-only-corpus.md).
 *
 * SCOPE ENCODING:
 *   Synchronisation reads TRUE scope-instance structure via herd's `tag2scope`,
 *   so a release/acquire pair synchronises iff (a) both are annotated at the same
 *   scope level S and (b) the two events actually share an S-instance. herd builds
 *   tag2scope only from a parseable `scopes: (sys (gpu (cta P0) ...))` tree in the
 *   .litmus body; the corpus now carries that full 3-level tree (emitted by
 *   generate.sh -- the diy `Scopes=(...)` info field is NOT parsed by herd), so
 *   tag2scope('cta|'gpu|'sys) resolve in every test.
 *
 *   Consequence (this is the point): the cta-scope test MP-cta-F is Allowed
 *   because its two threads sit in DISTINCT CTAs, so tag2scope('cta) does not
 *   relate them and the cta release/acquire is too narrow to cross threads. Put
 *   the same two threads in ONE CTA and tag2scope('cta) relates them, so the
 *   cta-scope sync fires and the weak outcome becomes Forbidden -- with no change
 *   to this .cat. Scope strength is read, not assumed.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * REPAIR LOG -- PORT2-R2 register item D14 (env-research/PORT2-R2-amd-oracle.md
 * section 7.D14).  D14 measured three defects that had to be closed before this
 * file could be cited as an instrument by `amdordercheck.py` Phase 2:
 *
 *   (i)   no SC-per-location axiom -- `coh-probe` (W -rf-> R -po-> W -co-> W)
 *         was Sometimes.                                  --> axiom (0) below.
 *   (ii)  fence blindness -- deleting every `f[...]` from the GPU-only fence
 *         tests changed 0/33 verdicts.        --> axiom (2) fence clauses + (4).
 *   (iii) no `'sc` set -- `strong` never mentioned the `sc` tag, so a system
 *         `sc` fence read as strictly WEAKER than a system release store.
 *                                             --> `SCT` below, axioms (2)-(4).
 *
 * The extensions are grounded, not invented, and their sources are NOT the
 * PLDI'23 artifact: there is no fence and no `sc` operation anywhere in the
 * artifact (R2 memo section 5.4.1, measured), so the "8/8 reproduces the
 * artifact" contract below CANNOT validate any of them.  It is re-established
 * after the repair (still 8/8) but it is silent about (i)-(iii); the probes in
 * hetlitmus/cats/probes/ and the cram test tests/cram/amd-cat.t are what pin
 * them.  Sources, per axiom, are cited at each axiom.
 *
 * Deliberately NOT changed by the repair (each is a knowingly weaker reading;
 * "doubt resolves toward Allowed"):
 *   - synchronises-with is built on `rfe`, where HSA-in-cat Fig. 3-15 uses
 *     `coh`  (R2/G3 disagreement A-D2);
 *   - scope pairing is EXACT-match, where HSA Fig. 3-9 uses scope INCLUSION
 *     via `active-events`  (R2/G3 disagreement A-D1);
 *   - the SC-order axiom (4) uses `(hb | fr | co)` exactly as printed in
 *     [SCATOM] Def. 27, NOT the transitive communication closure `coh` that
 *     env-research/g3-artifacts/amd-cdna3-candidate.cat uses.  Measured
 *     difference: `coh` would additionally forbid the 8 cumulative rows
 *     IRIW/RWC/WRC/WRC3-{gpu,sys}-fence.  Those need A-cumulativity of an sc
 *     fence, which Def. 27 as printed does not give, so they stay Allowed.
 *   - axiom (1) `no-load-buffering` stays as-is (R2 memo section 7.D4).
 *)

(* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *)
(* Communication. cos.cat generates the coherence order `co` (off by    *)
(* default for bell models) and derives `fr`; we add `rfe` and `com`.   *)
(* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *)
include "cos.cat"
let rfe = rf & ext
let com = rf | co | fr

(* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *)
(* Annotation event sets (from ptx.bell tags, via the cat primitive    *)
(* tag2events -- NB tag2set is interpreter-internal and not exposed).   *)
(*                                                                      *)
(* `tag2events` returns EVERY event bearing the tag, ACCESSES AND       *)
(* FENCES alike: ptx.bell declares `instructions F[{'acquire,'release,  *)
(* 'acq_rel,'sc}, scopes]`, so `f[release,sys]` is in REL and           *)
(* `f[sc,sys]` is in SCT.  That is load-bearing here (axiom (2)'s fence *)
(* clauses and axiom (4) both read it) and it is pinned by              *)
(* hetlitmus/cats/probes/seam-probe.cat + tests/cram/amd-cat.t.         *)
(*                                                                      *)
(* `sc` is BOTH a release and an acquire: [SCATOM] (Batty, Donaldson,   *)
(* Wickerson, "Overhauling SC Atomics in C11 and OpenCL", POPL'16)      *)
(* Def. 10 p.637 -- "acq = ACQ u AR u (SC n (R u F))" and               *)
(* "rel = REL u AR u (SC n (W u F))".  This is also the direction       *)
(* [HSA] (HSA PRM 1.2 section 6.2.1) takes for HSAIL `scar`, and the    *)
(* AMD-side mapping HIP seq_cst <-> HSAIL scar is R2/G3 A-D5.           *)
(* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *)
let SCT    = tag2events('sc)
let ACQREL = tag2events('acq_rel)

let REL = tag2events('release) | ACQREL | SCT
let ACQ = tag2events('acquire) | ACQREL | SCT

let CTA = tag2events('cta)
let GPU = tag2events('gpu)
let SYS = tag2events('sys)

(* ================================================================== *)
(* (0) SC per location.  D14 defect (iii).                             *)
(*     [HSA] Alglave & Maranget, "Towards a Formalization of the HSA    *)
(*     Memory Model in the cat Language", v1.2, section 2.2.2 p.22-24:  *)
(*     "SC per location is a property in most models studied ... and it *)
(*     appears in the HSA models described in 3 A cat specification of  *)
(*     the HSA memory model".  Figure 2-8 p.24 gives exactly this form: *)
(*         procedure sc-per-loc() = acyclic po-loc | com end            *)
(*     (Figure 2-7 p.23 gives the equivalent `consistent(complus,po)`.) *)
(*     Without it, `coh-probe` -- W1 -rf-> R -po-> W2 -co-> W1, which   *)
(*     every published model forbids -- was Sometimes.                  *)
(*     `po-loc` and `com` are the herd stdlib names (herd/libdir/       *)
(*     stdlib.cat: `let po-loc = po & loc`).                            *)
(* ================================================================== *)
acyclic po-loc | com as SCperLoc

(* ================================================================== *)
(* (1) Load->store program order: forbids load buffering (LB-sys).     *)
(*     AMD GPU lanes preserve R->W program order, so the LB cycle       *)
(*     (R->W po ; rfe ; R->W po ; rfe) is broken. This does NOT touch   *)
(*     MP (W->W,R->R po), SB (W->R po) or IRIW (R->R po): none use a     *)
(*     read-before-write program-order edge.                            *)
(*     KEPT UNCHANGED by the D14 repair: R2 memo section 7.D4 rules     *)
(*     that this file's contract is "reproduce the artifact 8/8", which *)
(*     needs LB-sys Disallowed, even though the SHIPPING CDNA3 oracle   *)
(*     departs from it.                                                 *)
(* ================================================================== *)
let load-store = (po & (R * W)) | rfe
acyclic load-store as no-load-buffering

(* ================================================================== *)
(* (2) Scoped release->acquire synchronisation (HRF so / hhb),          *)
(*     INCLUDING FENCES.  D14 defect (i).                               *)
(*                                                                      *)
(*     `rel-acq` is [HSA] Figure 3-15 p.46 (= Appendix B.2.4 hhb.cat),   *)
(*     verbatim in shape:                                               *)
(*        let rel-acq =                                                 *)
(*          ((W & Release) * (R & Acquire)) & coh                       *)
(*        | ((F & Release) * Acquire) &                                 *)
(*          ((po & (_ * W)); coh; (po? & (R * _)))                      *)
(*        | (Release * (F & Acquire)) &                                 *)
(*          ((po? & (_ * W)); coh; (po & (R * _)))                      *)
(*     with ONE deliberate weakening: `coh` -> `rfe` (disagreement A-D2 *)
(*     of the G3 memo, kept so the repair changes only what D14 asks).  *)
(*     Clause 2's target set is plain `ACQ`, which contains `F & ACQ`,   *)
(*     so the fence-to-fence case is covered without a fourth clause --  *)
(*     again exactly as printed in Figure 3-15.                         *)
(*                                                                      *)
(*     `inst(s)` is [HSA] Figure 3-9 p.42 `active-instance`, with        *)
(*     `active-events` replaced by plain `tag2events` -- i.e. EXACT      *)
(*     scope match rather than scope inclusion (disagreement A-D1,       *)
(*     unchanged by this repair; exact match is the weaker relation).    *)
(*                                                                      *)
(*     Reading the real scope tree is what discriminates MP-sys-F        *)
(*     (sys-instance shared -> sync fires -> Forbidden) from MP-cta-F    *)
(*     (threads in distinct CTAs -> tag2scope('cta) empty across them    *)
(*     -> no sync -> Allowed); two threads in one CTA would sync.        *)
(* ================================================================== *)
let inst(s) = let E = tag2events(s) in tag2scope(s) & (E * E)

let rel-acq =
    ([W & REL] ; rfe ; [R & ACQ])
  | (((F & REL) * ACQ) & ((po & (_ * W)) ; rfe ; (po? & (R * _))))
  | ((REL * (F & ACQ)) & ((po? & (_ * W)) ; rfe ; (po & (R * _))))

let sw = (rel-acq & inst('cta)) | (rel-acq & inst('gpu)) | (rel-acq & inst('sys))
let hb = (po | sw)+
acyclic hb as happens-before
irreflexive (hb ; fr) as coherence
(* D14 (iii) asks for `irreflexive (hb;co)` beside `irreflexive (hb;fr)`.
   It is NOT subsumed by axiom (0): probe S-probe-relacq is a cycle
   W -po-> W[rel] -sw-> R[acq] -po-> W -co-> W whose po edges cross
   locations, so po-loc never sees it.  [HSA] Figure 3-15 p.46 states the
   stronger `call consistent(hhb,coh) as HhbCohCons`; we take only the
   `co` half of it, which is what D14 names. *)
irreflexive (hb ; co) as coherence-co

(* ================================================================== *)
(* (3) System-scope release/acquire is STRONG.                          *)
(*     On AMD a system-scope rel/acq is a heavyweight flush+invalidate   *)
(*     to the system coherence point: store completion + multi-copy      *)
(*     atomicity, i.e. sequentially consistent AMONG sys-scope atomics.  *)
(*     This is what forbids SB-sys-F and IRIW-sys-F (plain release/       *)
(*     acquire alone would leave them Allowed -- their acquire loads      *)
(*     read the initial value, so the so-pairing of (2) never fires).    *)
(*     [CMCM] section 7.2 p.153:21 is the source, for the SIMULATED      *)
(*     GCN3 machine this file models: "with any system-scoped fence      *)
(*     (release, acquire or full fence), each of the litmus tests is     *)
(*     disallowed ... We refer to this memory model as Scoped XC."       *)
(*     NB the R2 memo section 7.D1 REVOKES this axiom for CDNA3; the     *)
(*     file is not a CDNA3 model and must not be read as one.            *)
(*                                                                      *)
(*     `strong` now contains the `sc` tag too (D14 defect (ii)):         *)
(*     seq_cst is at least as strong as release/acquire ([SCATOM]        *)
(*     Def. 10 p.637), so leaving `'sc` out made a system sc fence read  *)
(*     as strictly weaker than a system release store.                   *)
(*                                                                      *)
(*     Fences that land in `strong` are INERT here and that is by        *)
(*     construction, not by census: `strong-po` is transitively closed,  *)
(*     so a fence between two strong accesses adds no reachability, and  *)
(*     a fence carries no `com` edge so no cycle can route through it.   *)
(*     Fence ordering therefore comes from axiom (2)'s fence clauses and *)
(*     from axiom (4), never from here.  Probe SB-probe-2fence pins it.  *)
(* ================================================================== *)
let strong    = (REL | ACQ) & SYS
let strong-po = [strong] ; po ; [strong]
acyclic strong-po | com as sys-strong

(* ================================================================== *)
(* (4) The `sc` order -- the fence-ordering axiom.  D14 defects (i)+(ii).*)
(*                                                                      *)
(*     [SCATOM] Def. 27 p.644, "Proposed SC axiom for OpenCL", verbatim: *)
(*        acy(SC^2 n (Fsb? ; (ghb u lhb u fr u mo) ; sbF?) n incl)       *)
(*     with Def. 10 p.637 `Fsb = [F];sb` and `sbF = sb;[F]`.  Below,     *)
(*     `mo` = `co`, `sb` = `po`, `ghb u lhb` = this model's `hb`, and    *)
(*     `incl` is replaced by the file's exact-scope `inst(s)` (weaker,   *)
(*     see (2)).  Def. 27's point is exactly the scoped degradation this *)
(*     corpus exercises: "we do not need to restrict the programmer's    *)
(*     usage of SC atomics to certain scopes; instead, the guarantees    *)
(*     provided by those SC atomics degrade gracefully as their scopes   *)
(*     narrow."  Hence f[sc,cta] between threads in DISTINCT CTAs still  *)
(*     orders nothing, with no special-casing.                           *)
(*                                                                      *)
(*     This is the axiom that decides `2+2W-sys-fence` (pure `co` cycle, *)
(*     no rf edge anywhere, so axiom (2) structurally cannot see it).    *)
(*                                                                      *)
(*     UNANCHORED, and it must be reported as such: the artifact         *)
(*     contains no fence and no sc operation at all, so the 8-anchor     *)
(*     contract does not test one line of this axiom.                    *)
(* ================================================================== *)
let Fsb = [SCT & F] ; po
let sbF = po ; [SCT & F]
let scpairs = (SCT * SCT) & (inst('cta) | inst('gpu) | inst('sys))
acyclic (scpairs & (Fsb? ; (hb | fr | co) ; sbF?)) as sc-order
